Event-driven contaminant export and watershed predictability
This repository contains the data-processing and analytical workflow supporting the study:

Event-driven export reveals a watershed vulnerability-predictability gap

The study evaluates how hydrological variability organizes sediment and nutrient export across agricultural watershed monitoring stations and whether environmentally vulnerable stations are also hydrologically predictable.

Study overview
Monthly streamflow and constituent-load records from four agricultural watershed monitoring stations (LRDW, LRN, LRO, and LRO3) were analyzed from January 2014 to December 2020.

The dataset includes monthly observations of:

Streamflow
Total suspended solids (TSS)
Ammonia plus ammonium nitrogen (NH3_NH4_N)
Nitrate plus nitrite nitrogen (NO3_NO2_N)
Orthophosphate phosphorus (PO4_P)
Total phosphorus (P_total)
Chloride (Cl)
Dissolved organic carbon (DOC)
Total dissolved nitrogen (TDN)
Dissolved and total elemental loads
A total of 336 station-month observations were evaluated.

Research objectives
The analysis was designed to:

Characterize seasonal and station-specific hydrological regimes.
Quantify flow-load relationships and constituent-specific flow elasticity.
Determine how much contaminant export occurred during high-flow months.
Evaluate temporal export inequality using Lorenz curves and Gini coefficients.
Identify deseasonalized directional changes and structural shifts.
Compare simple and machine-learning streamflow prediction models.
Test whether contaminant-export vulnerability corresponds to hydrological predictability.
Analytical workflow
The repository implements the following analyses:

Data completeness and distribution assessment
Monthly hydrological and constituent-load climatology
Flow-duration analysis
Spearman flow-load associations
Log-log flow-load elasticity
High-flow export enrichment
Lorenz curves and Gini coefficients
Deseasonalized Mann-Kendall trend tests
Sen's slope estimation
Benjamini-Hochberg false-discovery-rate correction
Exploratory structural change-point screening
Rolling-origin streamflow forecasting
Vulnerability-predictability assessment
Machine-learning models
The following streamflow prediction approaches were evaluated:

Persistence
Seasonal-naive prediction
Random Forest
Extra Trees
Histogram-based Gradient Boosting
Predictors included streamflow at 1-, 2-, 3-, 6-, and 12-month lags, antecedent 3- and 6-month mean streamflow, and cyclic calendar variables.

An expanding-window rolling-origin design was used:

Data available through 2016 were used to predict 2017.
Data available through 2017 were used to predict 2018.
Data available through 2018 were used to predict 2019.
Data available through 2019 were used to predict 2020.
Model performance was evaluated using:

Nash-Sutcliffe efficiency (NSE)
Kling-Gupta efficiency (KGE)
Root-mean-square error (RMSE)
Mean absolute error (MAE)
Percentage bias (PBIAS)
Key findings
The analysis showed that contaminant export was strongly concentrated during a limited number of hydrologically active months.

The top 10% of flow months accounted for 85.9% of observed orthophosphate export at LRN.
The same high-flow fraction accounted for 59.9% of orthophosphate export at LRO.
High-flow months accounted for 53.4% of nitrate-nitrite export at LRO3.
High-flow months accounted for 46.9% of suspended-sediment export at LRDW.
Eight of 36 deseasonalized trends remained significant after false-discovery-rate correction.
Random Forest produced the strongest streamflow prediction at LRO.
Persistence remained more robust than complex models at some stations.
Contaminant-export vulnerability did not consistently correspond to hydrological predictability.
The findings indicate that export inequality and predictive reliability represent distinct dimensions of watershed risk.

